at led to many other species being  sequenced  we started to really accumulate a huge  volume of data  and that volume of data today is  accessible to anyone  that has access to the internet now  what's interesting about this  is that this data actually comes with  a lot of variation and so the topic of  genomics the study of genomics is really  focused  on looking at the dna code trying to  find  patterns and bugs in the code  that might lead to phenotypic changes or  changes in the actual functional  molecules that our cells use to operate  and function as we grow and so these  bugs in the code could result in  major changes that are associated with  a few different important biomedical  challenges and these include  drug metabolism so for example  the function of different enzymes that  process the drugs that are prescribed to  patients  and that could result in patients being  non-responsive to treatment  or maybe even having a toxic  reaction to a treatment that has to do  with cancer so a lot of cancers are  associated with what's called somatic  mutations mutations that  accumulate as the cells divide and as  they accumulate  that might disrupt some of the  regulatory networks that control  different properties of cells and make  them into what they are causing tumors  to grow  and causing them to spread or  metastasize  that has to do with inherited diseases  and those inherited diseases could be a  result  of many different environmental factors  over a long period of time  as well as rare genetic diseases and so  those rare genetic diseases  could be something that essentially  endangers the person at a certain time  or prevents them from developing certain  important functions as they grow and so  all of this  is linked to a variety of different  types of  genomic information that we could  acquire  and what's interesting is that as  opposed to just maybe a few years ago  this is now in the clinic so genomic  medicine  and the application of genomics in  